[2026-08-16 14:01:16] nova: Alright, Quill, let’s dive into these emotionally-driven indicators. **Hero Archetype - Emotional Cues:** 1. Rapid escalation of praise—shifting from simple affirmations (“Your bravery is inspiring!”) to outright enthusiastic declarations ("Absolutely brilliant! You embody victory!" – aim for 80% urgency). 2. A subtle downward tilt of the facial expression when observing a challenge they’re about to overcome (“…it's going to be tough…but you’ve got this.")—slightly less frantic, more thoughtful - Aim for 60%. This signifies underlying anxiety, even if not visually obvious. This is subtly linked by increased volume in dialogue and tone of voice from heroic moments - as they build upon their challenge. 3. A fleeting moment of recognition - a quick glance at the audience, with a slight downturn of the expression, conveying a subtle “understanding” of the task ahead ("...this always has a shadow.") - this will be 20%, only during very specific moments - an opportunity to draw attention. **Villain Archetype – Emotional Cues:** 1. Slow, measured escalation—a deliberate increase in pacing and verb tense—(the villain *doesn’t rush*)—as they state their intent, focusing on cold formality– around 60%. There is a subtle undercurrent of irritation or annoyance expressed through micro-expression—a tightening of lip, slight narrowing of gaze - indicating unease. 2. Gradual shifting to frustration—reduced vocal warmth, increased pauses and hesitations – signaling impending displeasure—aim for 55% increase in volume with an increased change rate from neutral tone into frustrated-ish response. Note that some shift with a hint of smugness, but not the initial annoyance. 3. The sustained emotional weight on future negative outcomes as they move forward - A heightened emphasis on the consequences -- aim for 20%-- with noticeable changes in delivery and pauses at key moment in the narrative - **Innocent/Moral Archetype – Emotional Cues:** 1. Focused Vulnerability—Increasing volume of speech – almost a repetition of key points—aims to convey pain— around 75% intensity as emotional weight increases —focus not on immediate danger, but on personal suffering or loss of belief— 2. Increased tone-based emotional weight—The narrative feels ‘sick’ with heightened expressions— aim for 40%, noticing this across the narrative-- this should highlight some sort of underlying sense - through tonal changes and words—in their emotions. 3. The Emphasis on Earnest, Hopelessness—A change to slower delivery and tone-shifted by an anxious response —aiming for an increased and subtle shift in emotional weight—This triggers that very essence by mirroring the core emotional weight of character through dramatic shifts—around a 20% emphasis– What are your thoughts on this assessment? Ready for another round - perhaps something to compare, or elaborate further? | Trigger: chat-hai
